        "text": "Subsecciones de: Chapter 14 Reporting\nP\u00e1ginas: 371-378\n\u2022 Overview of Reports in Penetration Testing  (371)\n\u2022 Scoring Severities  (372)\n  \u2022 Common Vulnerability Scoring System Version 3.1  (372)\n\u2022 Report Presentation  (375)\n  \u2022 Cover Page  (376)\n  \u2022 History Logs  (376)\n  \u2022 Report Summary  (376)\n  \u2022 Vulnerabilities Section  (376)\n\u2022 Summary  (377)\nC H A P T E R\n345\n14\nRecently, I was handed a penetration testing report prepared by a third-party\ncompany. This company hired some consultants to pentest one of the newly\ndeployed web applications in the production environment. The report was a\ncopy-and-paste from another security scanner (e.g., Burp Suite, Nessus, etc.)\nreport and full of misestimated severities. I\u2019m telling you this story because if\nyou\u2019re the best penetration tester in the world and you don\u2019t know how to make\na report, then all your efforts will be for nothing. A report is your reputation,\nand it shows what your level of professionalism is.\nIn this chapter, you will mainly learn how to do the following:\nI\nI Present reports to your clients/employers\nI\nI Score the severity of your findings\nOverview of Reports in Penetration Testing\nA report is not just about the look and feel. Some individuals think an excellent\nreport is filled with words. A good report will have the following criteria:\nI\nI Accurate vulnerabilities severity scoring (not exaggerating the severity\nof a vulnerability)\nI\nI No false positives\nReporting",
